Installation of the berry suite
===============================

Prerequisites
-------------

- Python 3 (this version was tested with Python 3.12, but it likely works with
  other Python 3 releases).

- A working installation of Quantum ESPRESSO, version 7.5 or higher, with its
  executables reachable through $PATH.  berry only supports Quantum ESPRESSO as
  the DFT package.

Python dependencies are installed automatically by pip; they are listed in
requirements.txt at the root of the repository.


Installing
----------

From PyPI:

    pip install berry-suite

Or, from a source checkout, run this in the directory containing setup.py:

    pip install -e .

Developer extras (twine, for publishing) are available as:

    pip install berry-suite[dev]

or, from a checkout:

    pip install -e .[dev]


What gets installed
-------------------

Installing the package puts two commands on your $PATH:

    berry        the main suite; runs every step of the calculation pipeline
    berry-vis    the visualization programs

Both take a subcommand.  Run

    berry --help
    berry-vis --help

to list them.  The scripts must be run in order, because each one consumes the
output of the previous one.  See the README for the pipeline overview, and
documentation.pdf for the full description of every option.


Noncollinear calculations
-------------------------

Noncollinear calculations need an extra step.  Quantum ESPRESSO's wfck2r.x
writes only the first component of the spinor, while berry needs the full
spinor in real space.  A patched version of that program, wfck2rFR.x, has to be
built.

The procedure (editing wfck2rFR.f90 and the PP/src Makefile, then compiling) is
described in the appendix "Installing for noncollinear calculations" of
documentation.pdf, in this directory.


Further documentation
---------------------

documentation.pdf (built from documentation.tex, in this directory) contains
the full manual: theoretical background, the input file format, and a
description of each program in the suite.
